WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.921
Hello everybody and welcome to today's NCBI
webinar.

00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:11.290
I am Peter Cooper and today's webinar is about
running the Prokaryotic Genome Annotation

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:18.760
Pipeline on your own data and with me today
is Francois and also Doug online and Doug

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.740
will answer technical questions.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.359
With that I will turn it over to Francois
who will talk about PGAP.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:32.160
Hello, my name is Francoise Thibaud-Nissen
and I am the team lead for the Prokaryotic

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:40.110
Genome Annotation Pipeline or PGAP at NCBI
and with me is Douglas Slotta who is the technical

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:46.100
lead for the project and he will be able to
answer questions that are more technical than

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.530
I can answer.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:54.940
The outline of the talk for today is first
we will review what the place of annotation

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.170
is in the biological analysis workflow.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:04.409
We will provide a brief description of what
PGAP is, the history and how it's derived

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:11.390
and we will spend a good chunk of time talking
about example start to finish annotation using

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:18.939
PGAP on your own data . Then we will give
tips on how to run PGAP on assemblies that

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:25.450
are what we call preliminary assemblies, which
means drafty drafty drafty assemblies.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.670
Finally I will go over a couple of features
in the works.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:37.130
Just as a reminder, PGAP is available on GitHub
and the link is at the bottom of the slide.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.130
(github.com/ncbi/pgap)

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:43.789
Genome annotation is a core component of many
whole genome analyses.

00:01:43.789 --> 00:01:49.700
Annotation of the whole genome is the determination
of gene location and gene function in the

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.039
genome.

00:01:51.039 --> 00:01:57.140
It's a precursor of many downstream analyses
including comparative genomics, phylogenetic

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:03.149
trees, exploration of biochemical pathways,
discovery of virulence factors and the list

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:04.760
goes on and on.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:11.030
I will argue it is also an important component
for the assessment of assembly quality and

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.750
we will get back to that point.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:20.129
So PGAP was started at NCBI 15 years ago.

00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:27.400
It is used to annotate almost all RefSeq assemblies
currently registered in GenBank which is over

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:32.709
170,000 assemblies from bacteria and archaea.

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:41.069
It is also used for the annotation of many
assemblies that are submitted and that are

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:43.430
in GenBank proper.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:49.019
it is a an automated pipeline that predicts
the structure of coding and non-coding genes

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:54.329
as well as providing functional annotation.

00:02:54.329 --> 00:03:01.920
So on the coding gene side, the core component
of the pipeline are protein homology.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:13.180
Most of the predictions are made using homology
to existing proteins with the help of HMMs.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:20.829
Another set of proteins are coded using GeneMarK
S 2+, which is an ab initio gene finder.

00:03:20.829 --> 00:03:26.459
We predict non-coding genes as well using
tRNAscan-SE or RFAM or Homology.

00:03:26.459 --> 00:03:35.100
That allows prediction of tRNA, small non-coding
RNAs, rRNAs and we predict crisper loci as

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.100
well.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:43.030
The functional annotation is done by a combination
of hits to hidden markov models to which we

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:50.040
have assigned curated protein names as well
as BlastRules.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:57.090
We also assigned function using the Sparcle
engine, we apply Conserved Domain architecture

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:03.939
with sparcle and as a last resort we will
use homology using blastp.

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:12.579
So the typical workflow at the moment is a
scientist will collect samples, culture the

00:04:12.579 --> 00:04:20.669
organism, extract DNA sequence the DNA and
assemble it and then submit to NCBI and request

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:23.889
we run PGAP on the genome.

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:29.580
So what we are looking at doing is flipping
the script a little bit and provide the ability

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:36.280
to users to annotate the genome before they
submit.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.450
If we do that the submission becomes optional.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.280
Why is that a good thing?

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:49.840
There are many reasons why you may want to
run PGAP yourself before you are ready to

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.180
submit or you might never want to submit.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:58.370
You may have proprietary genomes not ready
for publication or will never be published.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:02.830
You might be at the beginning of a project
for you try to quickly characterize your starting

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:08.000
materials and it has no place in the archives.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.889
Or you are trying to QC an assembly pipeline
you put together or have very large volumes

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:21.680
of data and submitting it to NCBI is too much
of a burden.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.340
And finally why would you use PGAP over a
different pipeline?

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:35.370
One reason to do that is that if you use PGAP
it allows much less noisy comparisons to PGAP

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:41.490
annotated RefSeq assemblies because you eliminate
the difference in annotation pipelines when

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.880
you do your comparison.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.710
All those are very good reasons that we had
been thinking about this for many years and

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:56.599
it has prompted requests from users over the
years.

00:05:56.599 --> 00:06:00.949
Finally about three years ago we started thinking
about how we could make a stand-alone PGAP

00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:04.940
pipeline that users could download and run
on their own.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:13.910
The goals were it would be publicly available,
that the results conform with PGAP run at

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:23.440
NCBI , the results would be reproducible,
it would be simple to use, it would produce

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:31.780
results compliant with GenBank submission
requirements and it would be easy to install.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:40.430
So in order to do this we re-implemented PGAP
as we have it at NCBI in the common workflow

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:45.020
language or CWL which is an emerging standard
for pipelines.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:51.960
We converted it from essentially an internal
pipeline language into CWL.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:57.940
We went through all the reference datasets
that are necessary to produce data identical

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:06.200
to what PGAP produces at NCBI and we package
a minimal set of reference data necessary.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:13.970
We added some validation of inputs in order
for the results to meet GenBank standards.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:21.139
So we validate the sequence format and validate
the size of the genome provided to make sure

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:28.009
they are within the range for the species
associated with the sequence.

00:07:28.009 --> 00:07:34.680
And we do run the sequences through vector
and adapter sequence detection.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.639
How is stand-alone PGAP packaged?

00:07:36.639 --> 00:07:39.930
A PGAP release is two things.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:47.270
It's a docker image that contains the pipeline
in CWL as I mentioned which is the glue between

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:48.810
the applications.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:55.340
It contains the binaries and also contains
the cwltool which is the reference runner

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.050
for CWL.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:05.419
In addition to that PGAP include reference
data located on S3 in AWS and contains custom

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:13.310
blast databases, HMM models and a taxonomy
database anything that is needed for the pipeline

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.860
to run properly.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:22.930
In order to run PGAP you need to have a computer
that has Linux, Windows, or a Mac OS X environment.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.710
You need to have Python version 3.5 or higher.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:32.560
You need to have installed Docker and you
need root access.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.790
You need a fairly large chunk of space a 100
gig is what we recommend.

00:08:36.790 --> 00:08:44.950
Keep in mind the reference data itself will
occupy 41 gigs of free space.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:52.660
And we recommend an 8 core CPU, 32 gig memory
machine, however you could go much lower than

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:58.250
that like one CPU and 12 gig minimum.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:07.060
So now let's go through an example from downloading
the pipeline to running the genome and looking

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:08.381
at the output.

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:12.700
First you want to choose where you want to
run PGAP.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:19.170
You can run it locally or you can run it on
a computer farm in your institution or in

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.250
the cloud.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.190
That really doesn't matter.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:30.110
The first thing you will do is download pgap.py,
the script that allows you to do pretty much

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:31.110
everything.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:37.670
To download the script you use curl in the
command line I posted on the slide or you

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:38.670
can use wget.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:44.100
After you get the script be sure to make it
executable.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.970
The next step is to download the docker image
and the reference data.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:55.680
For that you use that script pgap.py you just
downloaded, with --update and it will start

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.010
downloading what you need.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:04.640
The Docker image, and the reference data,
and will also extract the tar ball of the

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:05.650
reference data.

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:15.290
That takes a couple of minutes and you should
have a message saying downloading 100%.

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:22.310
In the directory you notice the reference
data is called input and then the name of

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:24.180
the release.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:30.750
It will also include some input data for an
example genome.

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:39.700
In particular Mycoplasma genitalium G 37 is
a very small genome and using the data from

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:47.029
that will allow you to quickly test that your
installation is working correctly.

00:10:47.029 --> 00:10:50.329
Now let's annotate the Klebsiella pneumoniae
genome.

00:10:50.329 --> 00:10:55.300
I picked a random genome that is Assembly
at the moment.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.490
What do you need for this?

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.410
How do you prepare the input?

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:08.579
The first thing we will prepare is a six line
text file which is really a pointer to 2 other

00:11:08.579 --> 00:11:10.180
files.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:19.260
The fasta file, I think you are familiar with
it, and another file of metadata.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:28.610
Both the generic YAML file the six-line file
and the metadata are in YAML text format.

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:36.690
The metadata included in the second file includes
the topology of the sequences.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:41.920
So the sequences in that multifasta file,
are they linear or circular and include the

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:48.540
genus species from which the genome is extracted
and it includes contact information for the

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:53.670
person submitting and an author.

00:11:53.670 --> 00:12:00.430
Now you have downloaded the pipeline and you
have to get the input so you are ready to

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:01.970
annotate your genome.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:03.260
This is a command line.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:09.850
So K_pneu.yaml is a small YAML file that we
prepared.

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:13.339
And there are two other parameters here.

00:12:13.339 --> 00:12:22.370
Dash r allows transmitting to NCBI that the
annotation has started and then when it is

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:24.540
finished that the execution has stopped.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:33.190
The reason we ask you to please use that parameter
is it allows us to essentially justify our

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.190
existence.

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:38.199
It allows us to show out bosses people are
using our tool.

00:12:38.199 --> 00:12:46.770
The second parameter -- I should say all we
collect is the start time, the end time and

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:53.050
the random UI/UD so we are not collecting
the final data that you get we are not collecting

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:54.850
the input data that you provide.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:57.940
It's very limited information.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:06.860
Also in the command line is the dash oh which
is where you put the output directory name.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:16.639
And if everything goes well you have the output
in the directory that we specified and you

00:13:16.639 --> 00:13:22.050
will find after a couple of hours for the
Klebsiella genome, about 3 hours, you will

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.180
get a message that PGAP completed successfully.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:33.149
One tip is if you are running remotely on
an ssh connection, we recommend you use screen

00:13:33.149 --> 00:13:39.500
or tmux or nohup to not lose your data if
your connection goes away.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:42.560
How does PGAP work?

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:46.450
What happens when you run the command line?

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:48.230
This is it.

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:57.820
So pgap.py will pick up the small YAML file
and the reference data and the docker image

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:07.279
and it will instantiate a docker container
in which the whole execution will happen.

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:13.209
So the first thing that it does pgap.py will
rearrange the input data that you have provided

00:14:13.209 --> 00:14:16.490
and insert the reference data.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:24.190
It will pass the input data to the cwltool
which is the CWL runner that takes the pipeline

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:27.800
instructions of what should happen.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:37.209
It takes the input and the pipeline and distributes
the job and runs step after step.

00:14:37.209 --> 00:14:39.000
Now let's review the results.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:45.490
The output format that you get should be familiar
to you if you are familiar with PGAP at all.

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:51.980
You have the multifasta file of the annotated
protein, you will get the annotated genomic

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:59.520
sequence in ASN format which is used for submission
if you desire to submit your results.

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:07.519
You will get the annotated genome in GFF three
format for loading to genome browser for example.

00:15:07.519 --> 00:15:12.970
And you also get the annotated genomic sequence
in GenBank flat file format.

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:20.589
Let's look at the output in the GenBank format
and this is what you would see if you're going

00:15:20.589 --> 00:15:30.970
to Entrez and type in any annotated genome.

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:33.110
The thing I will point out.

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:37.199
The input we provided are reflected in this
output.

00:15:37.199 --> 00:15:41.220
We said the sequences were linear and you
see that in the output.

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:48.970
We said the organism was Klebsiella pneumoniae
and the author was Peter Cooper and the contacts

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:52.040
are also reflected in the reference.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:16:00.259
Below you will see an annotation summary which
should look familiar if you have ever looked

00:16:00.259 --> 00:16:02.670
at genomes that were annotated with PGAP.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:08.300
The summary includes run information like
who is the annotation provider.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:14.149
Your name or your consortium name should appear
here.

00:16:14.149 --> 00:16:20.459
The date of the run, which pipeline was used
and in this case it was PGAP , and which software

00:16:20.459 --> 00:16:21.980
version was used.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:28.930
Below this the annotation results will appear
or a summary of the results, feature counts

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:30.210
essentially.

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:38.170
And one I want to point out is if we look
at the number of coding genes there are 2265

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.170
coding genes.

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:45.199
This is a very low number for a Klebsiella
genome we expect more in the range of 5550

00:16:45.199 --> 00:16:46.920
to 5600.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:56.449
If you look below you see a very large number
of pseudo-genes and the cause for that is

00:16:56.449 --> 00:16:58.160
that they are frame shifted.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:06.250
So that's a red flag on the assembly itself
and potentially indicates there's a lot of

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.820
indels in the assembly.

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:15.770
This is an important reason why it's great
for you to run PGAP before you submit.

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:21.590
What you should do instead of submitting is
you should review the results and submit them

00:17:21.590 --> 00:17:23.720
only if they are high-quality.

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:30.410
If you don't find the results to be satisfying,
that gives you an opportunity to go back to

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:37.160
your sequence or to the assembly and fix what
you think has gone wrong.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:44.930
We hope by putting PGAP in your hands , the
cycle of annotating, reviewing and going back

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:49.660
to the method will be much shorter.

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:54.240
A note on how you can run PGAP on draft assemblies.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:18:01.350
At the beginning of the talk I explained that
in addition to PGAP we validate the sequences

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:02.450
up front.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:09.060
We have the opportunity to ignore the validation.

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:17.040
For example the genome size check, maybe you
are just annotating a plasmid or maybe a portion

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:22.810
of the genomes or you don't know why but the
genome doesn't fit the size expected for the

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:24.160
species.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:30.790
You can ignore that with the --ignore-all-errors
flag.

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:35.910
It will also ignore invalid sequence due to
vector and adapter sequence those could be

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:38.680
cleaned up later if you wish.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.271
And you will also ignore stretches of N at
the end of sequences.

00:18:41.271 --> 00:18:46.920
The flag allows you to run on undefined species.

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:51.510
So you may have an idea the genus but not
the species.

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:57.920
This is convenient to get a sense of the assembly
quality early in the workflow.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:03.220
You should be aware that the result that are
produced may not comply with GenBank quality

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:04.220
criteria.

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:09.930
If you intend to submit results it's not a
flag you want to use.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.670
Moving on to what is coming soon.

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:17.010
There are a couple of things that we are at
work on.

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:21.480
The first is support for Singularity as an
alternative to Docker.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:28.560
The advantage of Singularity over Docker is
it doesn't require root access.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:37.170
So we still package the image in docker, however
when it comes to running the pipeline, the

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:48.280
container instantiated will be a singularity
container that doesn't require root.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:53.910
We are also planning to add a taxonomic verification
module.

00:19:53.910 --> 00:20:03.900
It is done by calculation of the average nucleotide
identity ANI which is a tool that GenBank

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:10.090
uses when evaluating the quality of assemblies
submitted.

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:18.760
That allows you to get one step ahead and
anticipate what feedback you will get from

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:20.420
GenBank.

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:27.030
Finally PGAP currently requires network access
and we are working on lifting requirements

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:35.140
for that so for people working in Biodefense
or have very sensitive data and don't want

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:40.860
the pipeline to access Internet at any point,
they can still run PGAP.

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:46.010
So more information is available on GitHub
and this is our site.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:51.910
You can find the PGAP releases as well as
documentation on installation and FAQs.

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:56.100
And very importantly there is a form to report
any issues.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:21:03.570
We really welcome any comments that you have,
whether it's feature requests or problems.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:08.920
We are committed to make your life easier,
so don't hesitate.

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:15.030
You can also look at open and past issues
to see whether other people have run into

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:18.380
the same problem that you have.

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:21.150
Finally I want to acknowledge all the members
of the team.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.070
Their names are listed here.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:26.760
Azat Badretdin and Douglas Slotta have done
the bulk of the work.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:32.510
Slava Chetvernin, Wratko Hlavina and Eyal
Mozes have also helped.

00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:36.470
Daniel Sorin is the project manager.

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:43.780
And Ben Busby and Lewis Geer have helped early
