00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:51.710
And Jim, Kim, Eugene have given their support
and I want to acknowledge Steve Turner who

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:59.100
was a user who was really motivated in having
PGAP.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:02.450
And I want to thank you for your attention.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:04.870
Now if you have questions it's the time to
ask.

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:06.970
[Peter Cooper] Thank you, Francois.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:12.050
A couple of questions came in and they relate
to other topics.

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:14.610
Someone asked about how to create the text
file.

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:19.750
They mentioned a bunch of different types
of text editors.

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:21.420
The main point is it just needs to be plaintext.

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:25.910
One question that comes from that is what
platforms can you run this on?

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:31.590
Can you run on Windows and Mac and Linux or
only available for certain ones?

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:36.970
[Francoios Thibaud-Nissen] It can run on any
operating system.

00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:43.970
Most of our experience is in Linux , but there
is possibility to run it on Windows and Mac

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:44.970
as well.

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:51.530
So this is the operating system, when I talk
about the operating system for the computer

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:55.660
you should know that container itself runs
in Linux.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:57.990
[Peter Cooper] Okay.

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:00.330
Thank you, Francois.

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:05.300
Also you mentioned the average nucleotide
identity and someone asked can you skip that

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:06.300
step?

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:10.920
And Douglas answered this into the question
pod.

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:15.760
And it is optional so you can choose to run
PGAP with ANI only or both.

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:20.890
Another question that is related to this idea
is is there any consideration of lifting the

00:23:20.890 --> 00:23:26.470
requirement so you could have an unknown species
but have genus so do you need to know the

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:32.293
genus or if you don't know what it is, is
there a way to go when you don't know what

00:23:32.293 --> 00:23:33.293
it is?

00:23:33.293 --> 00:23:34.293
[Francoios Thibaud-Nissen] You need to know
the genus.

00:23:34.293 --> 00:23:39.900
The reason for this is the genus influences
the set of proteins that align to genome and

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:48.090
essentially it influences the weight we give
to certain proteins.

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:55.870
We will up weight the protein that are references
called reference proteins that are annotated

00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:58.480
on the reference genome for the genus.

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:05.040
So if you change the genus, then we will up
weight potentially proteins that are completely

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.470
unrelated to the genomes that you are annotating.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:12.800
So really this is the idea for requiring at
a minimum the genus.

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:16.440
[Peter Cooper] Wouldn't that be a way that
ANI could come into play.

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:19.940
If you had something like that and didn't
know the genus it could possibly give you

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:24.130
the species but probably would give you a
genus?

00:24:24.130 --> 00:24:29.130
[Francoios Thibaud-Nissen] So ANI is not yet
in the pipeline but something we could envision

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:37.010
is you first run ANI to determine what is
in your sample what you sequenced and then

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:38.640
it would be a two step process.

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:46.240
First run ANI alone and then when you get
an idea of what the species likely is then

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:50.740
you would run PGAP with the proper species
that ANI determined.

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:53.200
[Peter Cooper] Okay, thank you.

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:54.680
Okay, thanks everybody.

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:57.810
I don't see questions right now.

